{"name":"Dust Bowl","hex":"#e2d8d3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Dust Bowl","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A geographical region of the American Midwest, extending into Canada, that experienced severe dust storms causing major ecological and agricultural damage in the 1930s.","The period of time in which these storms took place."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Dust_Bowl"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dust Bowl","blocks":[{"text":"The Dust Bowl was a period of severe dust storms that greatly damaged the agriculture and ecology of the American and Canadian prairies during the 1930s. The phenomenon was caused by a combination of natural factors, including severe drought, and human behavior, including the destruction of natural topsoil by settlers who did not use dryland farming methods to prevent wind erosion. The drought came in three waves: 1934, 1936, and 1939–1940, but some regions of the High Plains experienced drought conditions for as long as eight years. It exacerbated an already existing agricultural recession."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg/330px-Farmer_walking_in_dust_storm_Cimarron_County_Oklahoma2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust_Bowl"},{"title":"Dust Bowl (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Dust Bowl is the ninth studio album by blues rock guitarist Joe Bonamassa. It was released worldwide on March 22, 2011. The cover art is based on a famous 1936 photograph by Arthur Rothstein."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/2/27/Bonamasa_Dust_Bowl_Cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust_Bowl_(album)"},{"title":"Dust","blocks":[{"text":"Dust is made of fine particles of solid matter. On Earth, it generally consists of particles in the atmosphere that come from various sources such as soil lifted by wind, volcanic eruptions, and pollution."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Dust_storm_approaching_Stratford%2C_Texas.jpg/330px-Dust_storm_approaching_Stratford%2C_Texas.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dust"},{"title":"Bowl","blocks":[{"text":"A bowl is a typically round dish or container generally used for preparing, serving, storing, or consuming food. The interior of a bowl is characteristically shaped like a spherical cap, with the edges and the bottom, forming a seamless curve. This makes bowls especially suited for holding liquids and loose food, as the contents of the bowl are naturally concentrated in its center by the force of gravity. The exterior of a bowl is typically round but may vary in shape, including rectangular designs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/China%2C_Jiangxi_province%2C_Jingdezhen%2C_Ming_dynasty_%281368-1644%29%2C_Xuande_mark_-_Bowl_with_Decoration_of_the_%22Three_Friends%22_-_1953.631_-_Cleveland_Museum_of_Art.tif/lossy-page1-330px-thumbnail.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bowl"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Dust_storm_in_Spearman%2CTexas%2C_Wea01422.jpg/500px-Dust_storm_in_Spearman%2CTexas%2C_Wea01422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Dust_storm_in_Spearman%2CTexas%2C_Wea01422.jpg/1280px-Dust_storm_in_Spearman%2CTexas%2C_Wea01422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Dust_storm_in_Spearman%2CTexas%2C_Wea01422.jpg/500px-Dust_storm_in_Spearman%2CTexas%2C_Wea01422.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or